{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe current tests inspect replacement mechanics or final JSON rather than the write window (`test_applier_mapping.py:235-282`, `test_applier_provenance.py:155-185`), permit last-writer record loss (`test_bridge_state_atomic_write_concurrency.py:41-120`), and source-grep impossible-link durability (`test_impossible_links_cicada.py:467-493`). Live sidecars replace full snapshots in `peer_confirmations.py:192-217` and `impossible_links.py:376-401`; there is no existing lock-protected read-merge-write boundary to reuse.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd a focused shared `sidecar_transactions.py` owner for reconciliation JSON sidecars. It creates a stable sibling lock file for each target and holds an exclusive `fcntl.flock` on that lock file across the entire read, merge, durable atomic replace, and unlock sequence. The lock is deliberately not taken on the JSON file itself: `os.replace` changes that file’s inode and would break serialization.\n\nFor mapping updates, run each key-level mutation only after loading the latest valid mapping while holding the stable lock, then atomically replace the full JSON. For peer-confirmation and impossible-link stores, capture the records loaded at construction as a baseline and calculate an explicit delta at save time: upserts and deletions relative to that baseline. Under the lock, reload the latest valid records, apply the delta, and atomically replace the completed document. This retains independent keys and propagates deliberate impossible-link deletions rather than resurrecting them.\n\nDefine exact same-key semantics: the writer that acquires the shared lock later wins for that key, including a deletion. Thus the transaction order is the observable last-writer-wins order; independent-key deltas from either writer remain. Preserve old-or-new reader visibility through the completed-temp-file + replace publication protocol.\n\nA malformed or non-object JSON sidecar encountered under the lock must never be silently rewritten from an empty fallback. Leave its bytes untouched, emit a warning identifying the sidecar and skipped persistence, and let the reconciliation pass continue fail-open. Cover that disposition with `caplog`; valid later writes remain separately observable. Keep commit-back unchanged, but prove durability through a fresh store/process reader after publication.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/sidecar_transactions.py` (new stable-lock, delta-merge, atomic-publish owner)\n- mapping, peer-confirmation, and impossible-link sidecar writers\n- focused concurrency, mapping, provenance, malformed-state, and durability tests\n\n## Testing\n\nUse barrier-controlled independent writers, same-key writers, a delete-versus-write pair, and an in-window reader. Verify serialized lock ordering, no partial JSON exposure, malformed-file warning/no-overwrite behavior, commit-back, and a fresh-reader control.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `sidecar_transactions.py` uses a stable sibling lock file and holds its exclusive lock across re-read, merge, atomic replace, and unlock for every covered sidecar write.\n- [ ] Concurrent distinct-key mapping, peer-confirmation, and impossible-link updates retain both keys after the two writers finish.\n- [ ] A same-key upsert conflict resolves to the value from the writer that acquires the shared lock later; the concurrency test asserts that exact result.\n- [ ] A deletion relative to an impossible-link store’s load baseline is applied under the lock; a delete-versus-write test proves it is not resurrected, and the later lock holder wins a same-key delete/write race.\n- [ ] An in-window reader sees only the complete pre-transaction JSON or complete post-transaction JSON, never a partial document.\n- [ ] A malformed or non-object target encountered under the lock remains byte-for-byte unchanged, emits the documented warning, and does not fail the surrounding reconciliation pass.\n- [ ] Mapping and sidecar writes use the shared transaction owner rather than independent lock-free load/replace sequences.\n- [ ] A fresh store/process reader proves committed impossible-link and mapping state survives publication."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788375098631105001,"uuid":"824403d6-ffa2-41c3-9cdb-7113c8831bef"}